'''avulsion''' (ạ-vul′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''avulsio(n-)'', < ''avulsus'', pp. of ''avellere'', pluck off: see ''avell.''] '''1†.''' A pulling or tearing asunder or off; a rending or violent separation.
{{smaller block|The thronging clusters thin<br>By kind ''avulsion.'' ''J. Philips'', Cyder, i. 24.
On condition of everlasting ''avulsion'' from Great Britain.
{{right|''Jefferson'', Correspondence, I. 151.}}}}
'''2†.''' A fragment torn off.—'''3.''' In ''law'', the sudden removal of soil from the land of one man and its deposit upon the land of another by the action of water.

'''avuncular''' (a-vung′kụ̄-lär), ''a.'' [< L. ''avunculus'', uncle, dim. of ''avus'', grandfather: see ''uncle.''] Of or pertaining to an uncle.
{{smaller block|In these rare instances the law of pedigree, whether direct or ''avuncular'', gives way. {{right|''Is. Taylor.''}}
Clive had passed the ''avuncular'' banking-house in the city, without caring to face his relations there.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Newcomes, II. ii.}}}}

'''aw<sup>1</sup>†''', ''n.'' and ''v.'' See ''awe''<sup>1</sup>.

'''aw<sup>2</sup>''', ''interj.'' [Cf. ''augh, ah, oh.''] An exclamation of surprise, disgust, or remonstrance. [Colloq.]

'''aw.''' [(1) < ME. ''aw, au, ag, aȝ'' (''awe'', etc.), < AS. ''ag'' (''aga, agu'', etc.), that is, ''a'' followed by the guttural ''g''; (2) < ME. ''aw, au'' (''awe'', etc.), < AS. ''aw'' (''awu'', etc.) (or ''ǣw, eáw''), that is, ''a'' (or ''ǣ, eá'') followed by the labial ''w''; (3) < ME. ''aw, au, av'', < AS. (etc.) ''af'' (''av''); (4) of other origin.] A common English digraph (pron. â), formerly interchangeable in most instances with ''au'' (which see), but now the regular form when final, and when medial before ''k'', ''l'', and ''n''. {{smaller|Historically it}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller|etc.; (2), (3), ''a'' (ä) with an absorbed labial (''w'' or ''v''), as in ''awl'', ''claw'', ''raw'', ''awk''<sup>1</sup>, ''hawk''<sup>1</sup>, ''crawl'', etc.; (4) ''a'', ''au'', or ''o'' in other positions and of various origin, as in ''yawn'', ''yawl'', ''lawn'', ''pawn'', ''pawl'', ''bawl'', etc. In later words often a mere accident of spelling.}}

'''awa''' (a-wâ′), ''adv.'' A Scotch form of ''away''.

'''awabi''' (a-wâ′bē), ''n.'' [Jap.] The Japanese abalone, or sea-ear, ''Haliotis gigantea'', a shellfish found in great quantity on the southern shores of Japan and much used as food. {{smaller|The shell is extensively used for inlaying in lacquer-work, and in other ornamentation. See ''abalone''.}}

'''await<sup>1</sup>''' (a-wāt′), ''v.'' [< ME. ''awaiten'', ''awayten'', < OF. ''awaitier'', ''awaiter'', later ''aguaitier'', ''agaiter'', watch for, lie in wait, < ''a'', to, + ''waitier'', later ''guaitier'', ''gaitier'', ''gaiter'' (mod. F. ''guetter''), watch, wait: see ''a-''<sup>11</sup>, ''wait'', and ''watch''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To watch for; lie in wait for.
{{smaller block|<poem>Your ill-meaning politician lords,
Under pretence of bridal friends and guests,
Appointed to ''await'' me thirty spies.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 1196.}}}}
'''2.''' To wait for; look for or expect.
{{smaller block|<poem>Betwixt these rocky pillars Gabriel sat,
Chief of the angelic guards, ''awaiting'' night.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 550.}}}}
{{smaller block|All through life I have ''awaited'' the fulfilment of a prophecy. {{right|''Hawthorne.''}}}}
'''3.''' To be in store for; attend; be ready for: as, a glorious reward ''awaits'' the good.
{{smaller block|<poem>Let all good things ''await''
Him who cares not to be great.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Duke of Wellington, viii.}}}}
'''II.†''' ''intrans.'' To watch; give heed.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Awaitynge'' on the reyn if he it heere.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 456.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
(pret. ''onwōc'', pres. ''onwæcnan'', etc.), < ''ā-'', orig. ''on-'', + *''wacan'', ''wæcnan'', wake; (2) ME. ''awaken'', ''awakien'' (weak verb; pret. ''awaked'', pp. ''awaked''), < AS. ''āwacian'' (pret. ''āwacode'', pp. ''āwacod'') (= OHG. ''arwachen'', MHG. G. ''erwachen''), < ''ā-'' + ''wacian'', wake, watch. The above were prop. intrans., the trans. form being ME. ''awecchen'', < AS. ''āweccan'' (= OS. ''awekkian'' = OHG. ''arwecchan'', ''irwecchen'', MHG. G. ''erwecken''), < ''ā-'' + ''weccan'', trans., wake, arouse. The forms vary as those of the simple verb: see ''a-''<sup>2</sup>, ''a-''<sup>1</sup>, and ''wake''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To cease to sleep; come out of a state of natural sleep.
{{smaller block|Jacob ''awaked'' out of his sleep.{{right|Gen. xxviii. 16.}}}}
'''2.''' To come into being or action as if from sleep.
{{smaller block|<poem>Now with his wakening senses, hunger too
Must needs ''awake''.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, II. 178.}}}}
'''3.''' To bestir or rouse one’s self from a state resembling sleep; emerge from a state of inaction; be invigorated with new life; become alive: as, to ''awake'' from sloth; to ''awake'' to the consciousness of a great loss.
{{smaller block|''Awake'', O sword, against my shepherd.{{right|Zech. xiii. 7.}}
''Awake'' to righteousness.{{right|1 Cor. xv. 34.}}
<poem>And at his word the choral hymns ''awake''.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Don Roderick, st. 32.}}}}
'''4.''' To be or remain awake; watch. [Obsolete or poetical.]

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|Such as you<br>Nourish the cause of his ''awaking.''
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., ii. 3.}}
The purple flowers droop: the golden bee<br>Is lily-cradled: I alone ''awake.''
{{right|''Tennyson'', Œnone.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To arouse from sleep.
{{smaller block|I go that I may ''awake'' him out of sleep. John xi. 11.}}
'''2.''' To arouse from a state resembling sleep, as from death, stupor, or inaction; put into action or new life: as, to ''awake'' the dead; to ''awake'' the dormant faculties.
{{smaller block|My master is ''awak’d'' by great occasion<br>To call upon his own. ''Shak.'', T. of A., ii. 2.
Thou dost ''awake'' something that troubles me,<br>And says, I lov’d thee once.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid’s Tragedy, v. 4.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 2.''' To wake, excite, stir up, call forth, stimulate, spur (up).}}

'''awake''' (a̤-wāk′), ''a.'' [Formerly also ''awaken'', < ME. ''awake'', ''awaken'', < AS. ''āwacen'', pp.: see the verb.] Roused from sleep; not sleeping; in a state of vigilance or action.
{{smaller block|It is my love that keeps mine eye ''awake.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, lxi.}}}}

'''awakement''' (a̤-wāk′me̤nt), ''n.'' [< ''awake'', ''v.'', + ''-ment.''] The act of awakening, or the state of being awake; revival, especially revival of religion. [Rare.] ''Imp. Dict.''

'''awaken''' (a̤-wā′kn), ''v.'' [< ME. ''awakenen'', ''awaknen'', < AS. ''āwæcnan'', pres. used with strong pret. ''āwōc'' (see ''awake'', ''v.''), also ''āwæcnian'' (pret. ''āwæcnede'', pp. ''āwæcned''), awake; orig. intrans., but in mod. use more commonly trans.: see ''awake'', ''v.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To become awake;

readings.c3s2:
cease to sleep; be roused from sleep or a state resembling sleep: as, to ''awaken'' early.

{{smaller block|A music of preparation, of ''awakening'' suspense—a music like the opening of the coronation anthem.
{{right|''De Quincey.''}}}}
'''2.''' To come into being or action as if from sleep: as, hope ''awakened'' in his breast.

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To rouse from sleep or a state resembling sleep; cause to revive from a state of inaction.
{{smaller block|<poem>Satan . . . his next subordinate
''Awakening'', thus to him in secret spake.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., v. 672.}}
<poem>I offer’d to ''awaken'' his regard
For his private friends. {{right|''Shak.'', Cor., v. 1.}}</poem>}}
'''2.''' To call into being or action.
{{smaller block|Such a reverse in a man’s life ''awakens'' a better principle than curiosity. {{right|''Sterne'', Sentimental Journey, p. 77.}}
[''Awaken'' is chiefly used in figurative or transferred applications, ''awake'' being preferred in the sense of arousing from actual sleep.]}}

'''awakenable''' (a-wā′kn-a-bl), ''a.'' [< ''awaken'' + ''-able.''] Capable of being awakened. ''Carlyle.''

'''awakener''' (ạ-wāk′nėr), ''n.'' One who or that which awakens or arouses from sleep or inaction.
{{smaller block|Though not the safest of guides in politics or practical philosophy, his [Carlyle’s] value as an inspirer and ''awakener'' cannot be overestimated.
{{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 149.}}}}

'''awakening''' (ạ-wāk′ning), ''n.'' '''1.''' The act of awaking from sleep.
{{smaller block|<poem>Some minute ere the time
Of her ''awakening''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'' (some editions), R. and J., v. 3.}}}}
